{
  "thinking": "Four explanations to test. One coordinated run may have been split across job labels or clusters. Check overlapping allocations against escrow, cross-cluster traffic, and independent power data. A flat load may be batch inference. Check peer traffic and probe chips if the load persists into the live window. Several independent customers may have run separate jobs below the threshold. Check customer independence through the company register and compare job timing with interconnect traffic. Missing records or late payments may reflect routine errors. Compare scheduler records with escrow, bank payments, and utility energy. Start with the manufacturer's inventory, then compare it with the operator's system list.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Five explanations and checks. (1) Separate customers ran independent jobs below the limit. Compare escrowed schedules with customer checks and measure traffic between clusters. (2) Heavy usage came from batch inference. Compare provider outbound traffic with tap readings and any available live chip probes. (3) One training run was split across job labels or customers. Check for sustained cross-cluster traffic and use the balance checks to test whether the linked workload exceeds 50,000 GPU-hours. (4) Training ran on omitted hardware. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator’s system list and check grid energy for unexplained load. (5) Missing readings came from instrument failure or deliberate blinding. Check custody and compare the affected dates with independent grid or provider records. Start with manufacturer inventory as the independent baseline for the system map.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "An honestly reported mix of smaller customer runs could fit the claim. Check escrow, customer identities, and independent traffic for evidence of separation. A large batch inference job could explain flat power and a periodic line. Check for inference-scale peer traffic and sample live chips if available. One training run could be split across scheduler labels or clusters. Check for sustained cross-cluster traffic and use balance checks to assess its size and duration. A low-communication training run could look like batch inference. Compare sampled peer traffic with both workloads and use live chip probes where possible. Unlisted hardware could host an omitted run. Start with manufacturer inventory, compare it with the operator’s system list, and check any discrepancy against grid energy and instrument coverage.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The manufacturer confirms 2,000 GPUs were delivered. Compare that count with the operator’s list of clusters, GPU assignments, and feeds to identify any hardware that needs independent follow-up.",
  "action": "system_list",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Compare the operator’s system list with the manufacturer’s 2,000-GPU shipment record. Use the listed clusters and feeds to guide independent checks. If the counts differ, check whether the discrepancy reflects hardware out of service or an unlisted workload.",
