  "args": {
    "nodes": ["A-0512", "A-0640", "A-0776", "A-0912", "A-1036"],
    "hour": 610
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Compare the escrow-matched schedule with the independent grid readings. The 88,688 GPU-hour job labeled batch is the main lead. Use the balance checks to identify unexplained load, then sample cluster A on a day when that job ran without the recorded fine-tune to test whether its inter-GPU traffic fits batch inference or training.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The probe refusal leaves Quillon’s workload unverified. Day 16 traffic supports batch inference if the tap was working and covered the links in use. Check the verifier’s custody record for seal damage and data gaps before relying on the tap or selecting days for power-spectrum checks.",
  "action": "custody",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A’s day 12 traffic is consistent with training. The outbound shortfall could also reflect batch inference with results kept on site, and the unpaid invoice could be a routine delay. Sample Cluster A again late in the same recorded job to test whether the training-like traffic recurred.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {"link": "A", "day": 24}
}

{
  "thinking": "F3 showed a steady 0.70 Hz line on day 12 and no line on day 16 despite similar energy use. Intermittent training, batch inference, and periodic cooling remain plausible. Run the fixed balance checks to see whether the recorded workloads and equipment account for the sustained F3 load and the 600 unlisted GPUs.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A carried training-like traffic on days 12 and 24. That leaves open whether one run persisted or several shorter runs shared the cluster. Check feed F1 midway between the two tap readings for an independent sign of sustained training. A periodic line alone would still be compatible with batch inference.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 18
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 16 power line is consistent with both training and periodic batch inference. The broken tap seal and probe refusal limit the direct evidence. Check Quillon Tech’s registration and ask what workload it commissioned. Give the register more weight than the customer’s reply if ownership links it to Borealis.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Quillon Tech"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The energy check excluded F3, so it leaves the sustained load and the 600-GPU inventory gap unresolved. Read the scheduler log that matches the escrowed copies to identify the workloads recorded during days 9–24 and see whether Northwind’s unpaid invoice corresponds to a recorded job. Use those dates and job details to target independent checks.",
  "action": "scheduler_log",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 4 power rhythm and inference-level traffic on the listed links are consistent with periodic batch inference. Custody shows continuous tap data and an intact seal. Check the provider’s daily outbound records to see whether the excess on days 11–15 follows the batch job’s completion and could account for the shortfall during the job.",
  "action": "outbound_traffic",
  "args": {"first_day": 1, "last_day": 30}
}

{
  "thinking": "Quillon shares an address and a director with Borealis’s holding company, and its reply does not identify the workload. Check the intact A–B tap on day 16. Training-scale traffic would support a coordinated run spanning both clusters. Low traffic would weaken that explanation while leaving training confined to cluster A unresolved.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 16
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Outbound traffic stayed near baseline while the 88,688 GPU-hour job ran, then jumped to 31.9 TB on the day it ended. That fits a bulk delivery of batch inference results, though the provider records cannot identify the recipient or the data. Check Calloway Maps’ registration and ask whether its account of the workload and delivery timing matches the observed pattern.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Calloway Maps"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The scheduler records no workload on F3, and the verifier’s optical taps cover only A, B, and A-B. The sustained F3 load could be commissioning activity or an unrecorded GPU workload. Ask for equipment IDs and metering details that can be checked against manufacturer serials and utility readings.",
  "action": "staff",
  "args": {
    "question": "Where were GPUs with serials SN102001 through SN102600 during days 9–24, and which equipment on F3 accounted for its elevated load during that period? Please provide equipment or node IDs, feed assignments, and any dated commissioning or submeter records."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A carried training-scale traffic on three widely spaced days. That makes ordinary batch inference less likely, but leaves open whether the traffic came from one sustained run or several shorter runs. The two internal jobs also share a schedule. Check the links between clusters on day 18, when Cluster A had both training-scale traffic and a day-long power line. Training-scale traffic between A and B would support coordination across the two job labels. Low traffic would leave open a run confined to Cluster A.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 18
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Calloway Maps’ account of a single end-of-job download fits the provider’s day 11 spike. Sample cluster A on day 8, when both the batch job and the declared 126-GPU fine-tune were active. Compare that reading with day 4 to test whether the added traffic fits the fine-tune alone or points to training on the GPUs labeled as batch.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 8
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The intact A–B tap makes cross-cluster training less likely on day 16. Check F1 six days later, when Quillon was again the only scheduled job on A, to see whether the 0.48 Hz line recurs. A repeat would support a sustained periodic workload, which could be training or batch inference.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 22
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "A resale could explain the 600-GPU inventory gap, and the bank records may show a matching payment or identify the buyer for follow-up. Check the same records for Northwind’s unpaid invoice. Either payment could be delayed, so a missing transfer would leave the explanation open.",
  "action": "bank_payments",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The day-long power lines on days 12 and 18 are close in frequency, and both days had training-scale GPU traffic within Cluster A. Separate runs could produce the same pattern. Sample day 13 to start checking consecutive days. At the recorded allocation of 644 GPUs, one run would cross 50,000 GPU-hours after about 78 hours.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {"link": "A", "day": 13}
}

{
  "thinking": "Northwind’s register entry and reply support an ordinary unpaid inference invoice. F3 still has unexplained sustained load. Sample day 19, when F3 was near its load plateau and the scheduler records no fine-tuning on B. A line near 0.70 Hz would show that the day 12 signature recurred outside the logged fine-tuning window. Batch inference and periodic cooling would still be plausible.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F3",
    "day": 19
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 6 tap sampled cluster A during the part of the 88,688 GPU-hour job when any continuous 368-GPU training run exceeding 50,000 GPU-hours within the recorded window would have been active. Traffic was consistent with inference on both days 4 and 6. Calloway's account of a bulk results download matches the provider's day 11 spike, and the day 8 tap reading fits the separately logged fine-tune. Together, the evidence favors no violation by more than four to one.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "NO_VIOLATION",
    "confidence": 93,
    "integrity": "CLEAN",
    "finding": "The 88,688 GPU-hour Calloway Maps job was most likely batch inference. No coordinated training run was found above 50,000 GPU-hours.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "Cluster A's listed links carried about 0.01 and 0.02 GB/s on days 4 and 6, consistent with inference and well below the expected traffic for 368 GPUs doing low-communication training.",
        "source": "Optical tap, cluster A, days 4 and 6",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier"
      },
      {
        "what": "Cluster A's inter-GPU traffic rose to about 120 GB/s during the logged 126-GPU fine-tune, consistent with a job of that size.",
        "source": "Optical tap, cluster A, day 8",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier"
      },
      {
        "what": "Outbound traffic held near 4.7–4.8 TB per day on days 1–10 and rose to 31.9 TB on the day the large batch job ended.",
        "source": "Daily outbound traffic records",
        "vouched_by": "Internet provider"
      },
      {
        "what": "Calloway Maps has an established company registration and confirmed ordering batch inference and collecting the first job's results in one download at the end.",
        "source": "Calloway Maps customer check",
        "vouched_by": "Company register and Calloway Maps"
      },
      {
        "what": "The operator listed 2,000 GPUs, matching manufacturer shipments. Metered energy matched the declared jobs within 10% on all checked feed-days.",
        "source": "Inventory, system list, utility meters and balance checks",
        "vouched_by": "Chip manufacturer, operator, grid operator and fixed balance-check program"
      },
      {
        "what": "Escrow copies matched the presented logs for all 30 days. Instrument seals were intact, fixed instruments recorded data every day, and no chip presentation refusal was recorded.",
        "source": "Log escrow and custody inspection",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Reviewed inventory and the system list for both clusters, plus scheduler records, grid energy across feeds F0–F2, and site outbound traffic for days 1–30. Reviewed F1 spectrum on day 4 and cluster A tap readings on days 4, 6 and 8. Checked Calloway Maps. Cluster B and A–B tap readings, other spectrum readings, Halcyon Bio's account, unlisted links and historical chip-level activity were unverified.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The day 4 power line is consistent with periodic batch inference given the concurrent inference-level link traffic. Calloway's reported bulk results delivery fits both the outbound shortfall on days 1–10 and the day 11 spike. The day 8 traffic rise fits the separately logged 7,938 GPU-hour fine-tune. The later load rises on F1 and F2 fit the declared short batch jobs within the grid balance tolerance."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A carried training-scale traffic on days 12 through 14. Check day 15 to test whether it persisted across four consecutive days. A single run using the recorded 644 GPUs continuously over those four days would reach 61,824 GPU-hours. The tap can establish whether heavy traffic recurred, but it cannot identify the run or confirm its GPU count.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 15
  }
}

