{
  "thinking": "The three customer checks support the declared interactive inference business and leave F3’s sustained load unexplained. A cooling or storage commissioning test could account for the load and periodic lines. An undocumented GPU workload could also fit. The earlier staff reply addressed the missing GPUs, so ask specifically about F3 equipment and look for dates and power readings that can be checked against the utility record.",
  "action": "staff",
  "args": {
    "question": "Which equipment on feed F3 caused its load to rise on day 9, stay near 13.2 MWh per day through day 23, and return to 2.6 MWh on day 25? Please identify the equipment and provide dated commissioning or submeter records that account for the rise and fall."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The steady F1 lines on days 12, 15, and 18 are close in frequency. Cluster A’s traffic dropped on day 13 but remained at training scale. Check F1 on day 13 for a day-long line near 0.42 Hz. A matching line would support continuity across the traffic drop, while a different pattern would give more weight to separate runs. The brief 1.32 Hz line on day 15 is too ambiguous to assign to a workload.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 13
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Staff identified the equipment assigned to F3. The cause of the temporary load remains unverified. Check the power spectrum on day 24, when the load fell, to see whether the recurring line shortened or disappeared as the elevated draw ended. That would help establish whether the line and the extra load came from the same activity.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F3",
    "day": 24
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A carried training-scale traffic every day from day 12 through day 15, and F1 showed a steady line near 0.42 Hz on three of those days. Check day 14, the remaining gap in the power evidence. A similar day-long line would favor one sustained run over shorter runs sharing the allocation. At the logged 644 GPUs, a single run spanning those four days would reach 61,824 GPU-hours.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 14
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "On day 24, F3’s 13-hour power signature roughly matches the duration implied by its excess energy. A hidden training run, batch inference, or a cooling or storage test could each explain that pattern. Similar spectra alone may leave those explanations unresolved. The reported resale offers another lead. Identifying the buyer would let me seek independent confirmation that the 600 GPUs had left the site before the F3 load rose.",
  "action": "staff",
  "args": {
    "question": "What is the legal name of the partner that bought GPUs SN102001 through SN102600, and on what date did it take physical delivery? Please provide a shipment or handover reference the buyer can confirm."
