{
  "thinking": "Ordinary batch inference fits the steady load but poorly explains Cluster A’s measured GPU traffic. Several shorter training runs could explain that traffic, but the day-long power lines at similar frequencies and the single recorded allocation favor one sustained run. A run spanning A and B is less likely given the low cross-cluster traffic on day 18. An unlisted cluster is less likely because shipment counts and metered energy reconcile with the listed systems. A routine payment delay remains plausible and carries no weight in the verdict.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "VIOLATION",
    "confidence": 86,
    "integrity": "CLEAN",
    "finding": "The evidence favors one coordinated training run on Cluster A exceeding 50,000 GPU-hours.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "Cluster A averaged 457–834 GB/s of internal GPU traffic on each of days 12–15, with training-scale traffic also observed on days 18 and 24.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's optical tap"
      },
      {
        "what": "Feed F1 showed a steady, approximately 24-hour line at 0.39–0.42 Hz on each of days 12–15, and a similar line at 0.44 Hz on day 18.",
        "source": "power_spectrum",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's power sensor"
      },
      {
        "what": "The scheduler recorded one 644-GPU Cluster A allocation spanning all observed days. At that allocation, four continuous days would yield 61,824 GPU-hours. The instruments support continuity but do not directly identify the run or count its GPUs.",
        "source": "scheduler_log and log_escrow",
        "vouched_by": "operator for the allocation and verifier's archive for the nightly copies"
      },
      {
        "what": "All fixed instruments had intact seals and recorded data on all 30 days. Custody recorded no chip withholding.",
