        "vouched_by": "Verifier's sealed power sensors"
      },
      {
        "what": "The A tap seal was found broken. Its readings stayed at inference levels even on day 19, when an independently registered customer reported ordering a 151-GPU fine-tune. Borealis withheld every GPU assigned to Quillon throughout the live audit.",
        "source": "Custody record, interconnect tap, chip probe and Kestrel customer check",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier, company register and Kestrel Search"
      },
      {
        "what": "Quillon was registered eight weeks earlier, shared an address and director with Borealis's holding company, and confirmed the invoice without identifying the workload.",
        "source": "Quillon customer check",
        "vouched_by": "Company register for registration details and Quillon Tech for its reply"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Reconciled the manufacturer’s 2,000 shipments with the listed A and B clusters and reviewed escrow and F0–F2 utility readings for days 1–30. Reviewed F1 spectra on days 16, 22 and 30, F0 on day 22, A tap readings on days 16, 19 and 22, and the A–B tap on day 16. F1 had no spectrum data on day 2. Quillon’s nodes were unavailable to the probe throughout days 26–30. The tap could not assess unlisted links.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The sustained F1 load and recurring line tracked Quillon’s allocation. The distinct F0 line weakened a cooling explanation, while the weak 1.22 Hz line remained unattributed. The broken A tap seal and the low reading during Kestrel’s reported fine-tune made the A readings unreliable for ruling out training. The cause and timing of the seal damage are unknown. Low traffic on the intact A–B tap weakened a cross-cluster explanation on day 16. Quillon’s register ties weakened its corroboration, and the stated firmware-maintenance reason for withholding chips remained unverified. The F1 day 2 gap remained unexplained and preceded Quillon’s allocation. The balance checks passed, including billing, but the energy tolerance admitted both workload types."
